WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
&gt;&gt; Wandering around
a random forest.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.170
I won't get lost
because of StatQuest.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.650
Hello. I'm Josh Starmer,

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:14.265
and welcome to StatQuest.

00:00:14.265 --> 00:00:16.100
Today, we're going
to be starting Part

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.340
1 of a series on
random forests,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:19.980
and we're going to
talk about building

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.580
and evaluating
random forests.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.980
Note, random forests are

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.480
built from decision trees.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.200
So if you don't already
know about those,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.960
check out my StatQuest
and beef up.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:34.170
Decision trees are
easy to build,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.210
easy to use, and
easy to interpret.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.950
But in practice, they
are not that awesome.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.870
To quote from the elements

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.210
of statistical learning,

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.230
AKA, the Bible of
Machine Learning,

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.930
trees have one aspect

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.230
that prevents
them from being

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.390
the ideal tool for

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.045
predictive learning,
namely inaccuracy.

00:00:55.045 --> 00:00:57.440
In other words, they work

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.740
great with the data
used to create them,

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.720
but they are not
flexible when it

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.350
comes to classifying
new samples.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.340
The good news is that
random forests combine

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.720
the simplicity of decision

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.660
trees with flexibility,

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.140
resulting in a vast
improvement in accuracy.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.020
So let's make a
random forest.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.900
Step 1, create a
bootstrapped dataset.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.420
Imagine that these
four samples are

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:24.820
the entire dataset that

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.895
we are going to
build a tree from.

00:01:26.895 --> 00:01:29.150
I know it's crazy small,

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.010
but just pretend for now.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.210
To create a
bootstrapped data

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.610
set that is the same
size as the original,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.150
we just randomly select

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.470
samples from the
original dataset.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.170
The important detail
is that we're

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.330
allowed to pick the same

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.490
sample more than once.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.490
This is the first
sample that

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.295
we randomly select,

00:01:49.295 --> 00:01:51.050
so it's the first sample

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.385
in our bootstrapped
dataset.

00:01:53.385 --> 00:01:56.460
This is the second
randomly selected sample

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.475
from the original dataset,

00:01:58.475 --> 00:02:00.440
so it's the second sample

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.440
in our bootstrapped
dataset.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.840
Here's the third randomly
selected sample,

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.080
so here it is in the
bootstrapped dataset.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.020
Lastly, here's

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.260
the fourth randomly
selected sample.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.320
Note, it's the same as the

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.200
third, and here it is.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.040
Bam, we've created a
bootstrapped dataset.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.760
Step 2 for creating a
random forest is to

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.240
create a decision tree

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.165
using the
bootstrapped dataset,

00:02:30.165 --> 00:02:32.240
but only use a
random subset of

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.375
variables or columns
at each step.

00:02:35.375 --> 00:02:38.590
In this example, we
will only consider

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.770
two variables or
columns at each step.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.610
Note, we'll talk
more about how to

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.230
determine the
optimal number of

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.730
variables to
consider later.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.390
Thus, instead
of considering

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:52.950
all four variables
to figure

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.270
out how to split
the root node,

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:58.590
we randomly select two.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.790
In this case, we
randomly selected

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.130
good blood circulation
and blocked

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.430
arteries as candidates
for the root node.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.250
Just for the sake
of the example,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.430
assume that good
blood circulation

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:15.365
did the best job
separating the samples.

00:03:15.365 --> 00:03:18.420
Since we used good
blood circulation,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.260
I'm going to gray
it out so that

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.900
we focus on the
remaining variables.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.120
Now we need to
figure out how to

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.620
split samples
at this node.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.780
Just like for the root,

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:32.340
we randomly select
two variables as

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.760
candidates instead of

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.200
all three
remaining columns,

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.100
and we just build
the tree as usual,

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.540
but only considering
a random subset

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.660
of variables at each step.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.010
Double bam.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.340
We built a tree, one,

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.400
using a bootstrapped
dataset and two,

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.700
only considering
a random subset

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.040
of variables at each step.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.920
Here's the tree
we just made.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.740
Now, go back to
Step 1 and repeat.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.700
Make a new bootstrapped
data set and build

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.260
a tree considering
a subset

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.305
of variables at each step.

00:04:10.305 --> 00:04:13.720
Ideally, you do this
hundreds of times,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.640
but we only have
space to show

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.300
six, but you get the idea.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.640
Using a bootstrapped
sample and considering

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.260
only a subset
of variables at

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.940
each step results in a
wide variety of trees.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.260
The variety is what makes

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.120
random forests
more effective

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.730
than individual
decision trees.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.680
Sweet. Now that
we've created

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.600
a random forest,
how do we use it?

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.340
Well, first we get
a new patient.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:46.200
We've got all the
measurements,

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.320
and now we want to know if

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.780
they have heart
disease or not.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.280
So we take the data and

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.300
run it down the first
tree that we made.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:00.960
The first tree says,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.360
yes, the patient
has heart disease.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.675
And we keep track
of that here.

00:05:07.675 --> 00:05:09.870
Now we run the data down

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.970
the second tree
that we made.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.590
The second tree
also says yes.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:19.070
And we keep track
of that here.

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:23.585
And then we repeat for
all the trees we made.

00:05:23.585 --> 00:05:26.000
After running
the data down

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.160
all of the trees in
the random forest,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.480
we see which option
received more votes.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:35.650
In this case, yes
received the most votes,

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.000
so we will conclude that

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.240
this patient has
heart disease.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:45.305
Bam. No,
terminology alert.

00:05:45.305 --> 00:05:48.440
Bootstrapping the
data plus using

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:49.760
the aggregate to make

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.880
a decision is
called bagging.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.480
Now we've seen
how to create

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.200
and use a random forest.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.740
How do we know if
it's any good?

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.860
Remember when we created

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.260
the bootstrapped dataset,

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.360
we allow duplicate entries

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.820
in the bootstrapped
dataset.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:15.880
As a result,
this entry was

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.860
not included in the
bootstrapped dataset.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.680
Typically, about
one third of

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.140
the original data does not

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:26.100
end up in the
bootstrapped dataset.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.620
Here's the entry
that didn't

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.420
end up in the
bootstrapped dataset.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.830
If the original
dataset were larger,

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.210
we'd have more than just
one entry over here.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:42.550
This is called the
out-of-bag dataset.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:45.190
If it were up to
me, I would have

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.530
named it the
out-of-boot dataset,

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.130
since it's the
entries that didn't

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.090
make it into the
bootstrapped dataset.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:53.590
Unfortunately,
it's not up to me.

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:55.770
Since the out-of-bag data

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.250
was not used to
create this tree,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.030
we can run it through
and see if it correctly

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:04.690
classifies the sample
as no heart disease.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.070
In this case, the tree

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:10.820
correctly labels the
out-of-bag sample, no.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:13.940
Then we run this
out-of-bag sample

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:15.480
through all of
the other trees

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.360
that were built
without it.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.000
This tree
incorrectly labeled

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.600
the out-of-bag
sample, yes.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.220
These trees
correctly labeled

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.680
the out-of-bag sample, no.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.000
Since the label with
the most votes wins,

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.920
it is the label
that we assign

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:34.880
this out-of-bag sample.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.400
In this case,
the out-of-bag

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.040
sample is
correctly labeled

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.960
by the random forest.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.000
We then do the same
thing for all of the

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.800
other out-of-bag
samples for

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.520
all of the trees.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.840
This out-of-bag sample

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.605
was also correctly
labeled.

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:55.620
This out-of-bag sample was

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:02.090
incorrectly labeled, etc.

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:04.980
Ultimately, we
can measure how

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.400
accurate our random
forest is by

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.780
the proportion of
out-of-bag samples that

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:10.580
were correctly

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.120
classified by the
random forest.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.680
The proportion of

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.320
out-of-bag
samples that were

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.060
incorrectly classified

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.880
is the out-of-bag error.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:24.820
We now know how to one,

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:26.560
build a random forest,

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:29.020
two, use a random forest,

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:31.720
and three, estimate
the accuracy

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:33.800
of a random forest.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:37.800
However, now that we
know how to do this,

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:39.640
we can talk a little

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.760
more about how to do this.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:43.880
Remember when we built

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:46.740
our first tree and we
only use two variables,

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.280
columns of data,

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.640
to make a decision
at each step,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.800
now we can compare the
out-of-bag error for

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.560
a random forest
built using

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.190
only two variables
per step,

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:00.560
to a random forest built

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:03.340
using three
variables per step,

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:06.220
and we test a bunch
of different settings

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:09.620
and choose the most
accurate random forest.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.960
In other words, one,

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.900
we build a random forest,

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.540
and then two, we

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.320
estimate the accuracy
of a random forest.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.320
Then we change
the number of

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.400
variables used per step,

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.580
and we do this a
bunch of times and

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:25.660
then choose the one

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.700
that is the most accurate.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.020
Typically, we start by

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:31.840
using the square
of the number of

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:33.500
variables and then try

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.095
a few settings above
and below that value.

00:09:37.095 --> 00:09:40.105
Triple bam.

00:09:40.105 --> 00:09:42.420
Hooray. We've made it to

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.945
the end of another
exciting StatQuest.

00:09:44.945 --> 00:09:46.020
Tune in next week,

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.340
and we'll talk
about how to deal

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:48.540
with missing data and

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.540
how to cluster
the samples.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.060
Until then, quest on.

